yield: for 1kg meat, 500g each — about 40 skewers, serves 4-6
uses:
  - satay-peanut-sauce
ingredients:
  - item: chicken thigh
    mass: 500
    basis: true
    note: boneless, skin off, 2.5cm cubes — keep the fat on
  - item: beef rump
    mass: 500
    note: or topside, 2cm cubes cut against the grain
components:
  - name: rempah (marinade)
    ingredients:
      - item: lemongrass
        mass: 40
        qty: 3
        unit: stalk
        note: white part only, sliced thin
      - item: shallots
        mass: 80
        note: asian shallots
      - item: garlic
        mass: 15
        qty: 4
        unit: clove
      - item: galangal
        mass: 20
      - item: fresh turmeric
        mass: 15
        note: sub 5g ground
      - item: candlenuts
        mass: 20
        qty: 4
        unit: ea
        optional: true
        note: sub macadamia
      - item: coriander seed
        mass: 8
        qty: 3
        unit: tsp
      - item: cumin seed
        mass: 3
        qty: 1.5
        unit: tsp
      - item: fennel seed
        mass: 3
        qty: 1.5
        unit: tsp
      - item: gula melaka
        mass: 60
        note: palm sugar; sub dark brown sugar
      - item: salt
        mass: 12
      - item: neutral oil
        mass: 40
    steps:
      - toast the coriander, cumin and fennel in a dry pan until fragrant, then grind
      - blend lemongrass, shallots, garlic, galangal, turmeric and candlenuts to a fine paste
      - stir the ground spices, gula melaka, salt and oil through the paste
  - name: chicken
    steps:
      - thigh, not breast — breast dries out over coals before it takes any colour
      - 2.5cm cubes, fat left on; it bastes the skewer as it renders
      - marinate 4 hrs to overnight
      - thread 4-5 pieces tight, leaving a handle
      - grill through, 2-3 mins a side
  - name: beef
    steps:
      - rump or topside, 2cm cubes cut against the grain
      - marinate overnight — beef takes the rempah more slowly than chicken
      - thread 4-5 pieces tight
      - grill hotter and faster than the chicken and pull at medium; beef satay goes tough well before it goes dry
  - name: basting oil
    ingredients:
      - item: neutral oil
        mass: 60
      - item: reserved marinade
        mass: 30
      - item: lemongrass stalks
        qty: 3
        unit: ea
        note: bruised at one end to make a brush
    steps:
      - combine oil and marinade in a bowl
      - smash the ends of the lemongrass stalks flat and tie them into a brush
      - baste at every turn — the brush is not just for show, it lays down aromatics that char onto the meat
  - name: grilling
    steps:
      - soak bamboo skewers 30 mins minimum, or they burn through
      - charcoal if you have it; this is a dish built around smoke and direct radiant heat
      - grill screaming hot and fast, close to the coals
      - keep them moving — the gula melaka means they go from caramelised to burnt quickly
      - you want blackened spots and sticky edges, not an even brown or a uniform char
      - shield the exposed skewer ends with foil if they catch
  - name: serving
    ingredients:
      - item: cucumber
        note: 2cm chunks, raw
      - item: red onion
        note: 2cm chunks, raw
      - item: ketupat
        optional: true
        note: compressed rice cakes; plain steamed rice pressed and cut works
    steps:
      - pile the skewers up, peanut sauce in a bowl alongside for dipping
      - raw cucumber and onion chunks on the side — they're the palate cleanser between sticks, not a garnish
notes: |
  Singapore hawker style, so rempah-driven and caramelised rather than the pale
  coconut-milk-and-turmeric Thai version. The marinade has no coconut milk in it at all.

  Gula melaka does most of the work. It's what gives the char, the sticky edges and the
  smell — cut it back and you get spiced grilled meat, not satay. It's also why the grill
  has to be fast and attended.

  Same rempah for both meats. The difference is cut, marinating time and how you grill them:
  chicken through, beef pulled at medium.

  Fresh turmeric over ground if you can get it, and fresh galangal over the jarred stuff.
  Neither has a good substitute — ginger is not galangal.

  Scaling assumes the 50:50 chicken:beef split. Change the ratio and set the rempah against
  the total meat weight instead.
